---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FounderPhoto from '../components/FounderPhoto.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const CONTACT_EMAIL = SITE.email;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Work with me — TomProtects"
  description="Security advice for small practices, nonprofits and local government agencies. What the work is, who it fits, and how to start a conversation. Morgantown, West Virginia, and remote."
>
  <Section width="text">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Work with me</h1>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        I help small organizations build their first real security program: work out where you
        stand, decide what to do first, and keep it moving once it exists.
      </p>

      <p>
        Engagements vary, but they draw on the same
        <a href="/#how-i-help" class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4"
          >four areas</a
        >: understanding where you stand, knowing what to do next, keeping it moving, and handling
        newer risks calmly. Some organizations want one assessment and a roadmap they can run
        themselves. Others want someone in the room month to month. Both are reasonable places to
        start.
      </p>

    </div>

    <Callout on="cream" class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="text-ink/80">
        The work is advisory. I do not sell products and I do not take over your systems.
        Implementation happens through the IT staff or provider you already have, who know your
        environment. My part is making sure the right things happen, in the right order.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Who it fits</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Small organizations, roughly five to twenty-five people, that depend on technology for
        nearly everything they do, have nobody whose actual job is security, and have started
        fielding questions from clients, insurers, regulators or a board that are difficult to
        answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most often that is a small professional practice — dental, medical, legal, accounting — a
        small nonprofit, or a small local government agency. Different work, same position: real
        responsibility for information other people trusted you with, and nobody inside whose job
        it is to decide what to do about it.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is worth saying what does not fit, too. If you are much larger than this — big enough
        that security is already someone's actual job — or you already have a security team, or
        what you need is a penetration test or a certification against a fixed deadline, this is
        not the right engagement. I would rather tell you that in the first ten minutes than three
        weeks in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!--
    Engagement shape. Cream, which is why the booking band below it is now white
    rather than inheriting the page ground — inserting a band here would
    otherwise put two cream bands back to back. The order matters more than the
    grounds: what an engagement costs and how it is shaped belongs before the
    calendar, not after it.
  -->
  <Section width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">How an engagement starts</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Almost every engagement begins the same way, with a fixed-price starting assessment. The
        scope is set before it begins and so is the cost, so there is no meter running and no
        surprise at the end. You get an accurate picture of where the organization stands, and a
        prioritized roadmap: what to do now, what can wait, and what you can reasonably set aside.
      </p>

      <p>
        That assessment stands on its own. If you would rather take the roadmap and run it with the
        IT provider you already have, that is a perfectly good outcome — it is written to be handed
        over.
      </p>

      <p>
        For organizations that would rather not carry it alone, ongoing advisory is available
        afterwards — month to month, keeping the roadmap current and staying reachable when a
        decision comes up. It is offered after the assessment rather than bundled into it, because
        you should be able to see the work before deciding whether you want more of it.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/*
      The one price on the site. A Panel rather than a Callout: a Callout is an
      aside set apart from the copy, and this is not an aside — it is the answer
      to the question a reader arrives with, and it should be findable by
      someone scanning the page rather than reading it.

      font-serif is named explicitly. The base layer only puts Fraunces on
      h1-h4, and this is a <p>: it carries display weight without claiming a
      place in the heading structure, the same way <PullQuote> does.
    */}
    <Card radius="2xl" padding="lg" class="mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      <p class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
        Security foundation assessment — $3,500
      </p>
    </Card>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-ink/70">
      The assessment is advisory: it does not include penetration testing, remediation work, or
      ongoing monitoring.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">
      Why not just buy a one-off audit from a security firm?
    </h3>

    <div class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        It is a reasonable thing to buy, and for some organizations it is the right thing. What it
        usually produces is a report, written to be thorough, and handed over at the point where
        the actual work starts. If you have someone in-house who can turn a hundred findings into
        the four things worth doing this quarter, that is a good trade. If you do not, the report
        becomes one more thing nobody has time to read, and a year later the same questions are
        still open. The assessment here is not a better audit — it is an assessment that arrives
        already sorted into an order you can act on, from someone who is still around afterwards to
        answer what comes up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Starting a conversation</h2>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-ink/70">
      A first call is about where your organization stands today, what is already being asked of
      it, and whether an engagement would help. No obligation either way.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink/70">
      I'm based in Morgantown, West Virginia. Most of the organizations I work with are around
      north-central West Virginia, close enough to meet in person when that is the useful thing to
      do, and I work remotely with those further afield. Either way the call is the same.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex items-center gap-4">
      <FounderPhoto class="h-16 w-16 shrink-0" />
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/70">
        You'll be talking to me directly — Tom. CISSP, CISM, CDPSE and PMP, and more than twenty
        years in security.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-3 text-ink/80">
      {/*
        Deliberately no duration here. The calendar below is booked as a
        30-minute slot, and the v1 copy this page replaced said 15–20, so the
        two contradicted each other on the same screen. Whichever is right, it
        belongs in TidyCal — stating it twice only means it can drift again.
      */}
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A short call, no pressure, no pitch
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> Plain-English answers to your security
        questions
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 font-bold text-accent">✓</span> A clear sense of whether an engagement
        would help, and what it would involve
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- TidyCal scheduling embed — unchanged: same account, same path, same markup. -->
    <Card radius="2xl" padding="xs" surface="cream" class="mt-10 sm:p-4">
      <div class="tidycal-embed" data-path="tomsmith/discovery"></div>
      <script is:inline src="https://asset-tidycal.b-cdn.net/js/embed.js" async></script>
      <noscript>
        <p class="p-4 text-ink/70">
          The booking calendar needs JavaScript. You can also book directly at
          <a href="https://tidycal.com/tomsmith/discovery" class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4">tidycal.com/tomsmith/discovery</a>.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </Card>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-sm text-ink/70">
      Would rather write than book a slot? Email
      <a
        href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
        class="font-medium text-accent underline underline-offset-4">{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
      > and tell me where things stand.
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
